    <string name="halo11_coach_divide">Share into equally sized groups</string>
    <string name="halo11_coach_acceleration_idea">Divide the change in speed by the time</string>
    <string name="halo11_coach_ones_add">When you put the ones together, do you make a new ten?</string>
    <string name="halo11_coach_ones_sub">Do you need to trade one ten for ten ones before subtracting?</string>
    <string name="halo11_coach_smaller">Start by circling the ones. Work on that part first.</string>
    <string name="halo11_coach_smaller_add">Write the two groups separately. Then bring them together on paper.</string>
    <string name="halo11_coach_smaller_sub">Draw the first group. Cross out the amount taken away.</string>
    <string name="halo11_coach_smaller_multiply">Make one row for each group. Use the same number in every row.</string>
    <string name="halo11_coach_smaller_divide">Draw places for the groups. Give each group one item in turn.</string>
    <string name="halo11_coach_smaller_acceleration">Find the change in speed first. Then divide by the time in seconds.</string>
    <string name="halo11_coach_ones_illustration">Ones in the first number\n%1$s</string>
    <string name="halo11_coach_explain_add">Line up matching place values. Add the ones first and carry each new group of ten to the next column.</string>
    <string name="halo11_coach_explain_subtract">Line up matching place values. If the top column has too few ones, trade one ten for ten ones before subtracting.</string>
    <string name="halo11_coach_explain_multiply">Use one factor for the number of groups and the other for items per group. Add those equal groups.</string>
    <string name="halo11_coach_explain_divide">Use the divisor as the number of equal groups. The quotient is the number of items in each group.</string>
    <string name="halo11_coach_explain_acceleration">Subtract the initial speed from the final speed to find the change in speed. Dividing m/s by seconds gives m/s².</string>
